  "strategy": "Approach and rationale:\nWe aim to improve upon the current record of 0.381432 for the Erd\u0151s constant by employing a multi-objective optimization strategy combined with geometry-inspired search. Unlike previous methods that primarily relied on gradient-based local search, this approach introduces:\n\n1. **Global Optimization**: Uses Bayesian optimization to explore the solution space more effectively by building a probabilistic model of the objective function and using acquisition functions to balance exploration and exploitation.\n\n2. **Geometry-inspired moves**: Implements search moves that mimic geometric transformations (triangulation, reflection) to escape local optima, leveraging the structure of the optimization landscape.\n\n3. **Progressive resolution**: Starts with modest resolution (200 samples) for rapid exploration, then refines to higher resolutions (400-800 samples) for closer-to-optimum solutions.\n\n4. **Multi-strategy combination**: Combines gradient descent, Bayesian optimization, and geometric search, each activated at different stages of the optimization process.\n\nThe primary bottleneck addressed is the slow convergence in high dimensions, typical of single-strategy optimization. This approach uses a mathematical programming formulation with smoothing and requires no derivative information.\n\nMathematical Specification:\nMinimize C\u2085(h) = (\u220f_{k=-n+1}^{n-1} \u27e8h \u00b7 (1 - h(k\u00b7))\u27e9)^{1/2} \nsubject to:\n0 \u2264 h(x) \u2264 1 for all x \u2208 [0,2]\n\u222b\u2080\u00b2 h(x) dx = 1\n\nDiscretization with n samples on [0,2] with dx=2.0/n\nObjective evaluated via: C\u2085 = max_k corr_coeffs(h,1\u2212h,k) * dx\n\nImplementation Procedure:\n\n1. **Input Handling**:\n   - Read parent vector if available\n   - Allow mixed resolution handling\n\n2. **Resolution Conversion**:\n   - Handle parent vector (n\u2080=100) if provided\n   - Project to target n (100,400,800)\n   - Normalize to sum 0.5dx (expect sum 100)\n\n3. **Objective Calculation**:\n   - Automatically compute autocorrelation across all lags\n   - Handle non-circular bounds correctly\n\n4. **Repair Function**:\n   - Clip values to [0,1]\n   - Exact sum enforcement via constrained distribution\n\n5. **Optimizer Setup**:\n   - Bayesian Optimization kernel\n   - Geometry-based adaptive sampling\n   - Gradient-free though probabilistic\n\n6. **Search Phases**:\n   - Exploration (20% budget): Bayesian optimization\n   - Refinement (60% budget): Geometric moves & gradient-based\n   - Localization (20% budget): Probabilistic localization\n\nSearch Choices and Variations:\n\n1. Resolution Sweep (400 to 800 with 200 fallback)\n2. Knowledge base transfer between scales\n3. Bayesian vs. random balance (exploitation-exploration)\n4. Progressive n_res adjustment during optimization\n\nBudget and Robustness:\n\n- Adjustable resolution handles trade-offs\n- Provided parent vector fallback for restarts\n- Wall time monitoring for timely termination\n- Priority to least time-constrained solutions first\n\nValidation and Return Contract:\n\n1. Feasibility checks:\n   - 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n   - Sum(h) \u2248 n/2 within 1e-5\n   - Objective evaluations via discrete convolution\n\n2. Return candidates are repaired and evaluated with:\n   - Primary diagnosis score: C\u2085(h)\n   - Shape features to detect valid configurations\n\nThe algorithm automatically down-samples inferior candidates and intelligently redistributes budget.",
